    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d417">

                <pb facs="1159" n="1138" sameAs="frus:frus1961-63v13/pg1138"/>

                <head>417. Message From President Johnson to Prime Minister Home<anchor n="0" xml:id="d417-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1963-12-24">December 24,
                        1963</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Prime Minister</hi>: Thank you very much for
                    your note of December 20<anchor n="1" xml:id="d417-f2"/> about the
                    understandings which have existed among our predecessors on the use of nuclear
                    weapons. In a general way the understandings seem reasonable to me, but I would
                    like to take advantage of your suggestion that we might settle this matter
                    definitely when we <pb facs="1160" n="1139" xml:id="pg1139"/> meet. I am taking
                    particular care with all matters affecting decisions on nuclear weapons, as I am
                    sure you are too, and it will be a help to me not to make any mere formal
                    decision on this point until February. Meanwhile, of course, it is clear (1)
                    that no U.S. nuclear weapons will be used from British territory without your
                    consent, and (2) that we should consult if possible before either of us uses
                    them anywhere.</p>
                <p>Sincerely,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Lyndon B.
                                Johnson</hi><anchor n="2" xml:id="d417-f3"
                    /></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d417-f1" n="0" type="source" xml:id="d417-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Johnson Library, National Security
                    File, Aides Files, Bundy. Top Secret. Sent
                    by private channel.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d417-f2" n="1" xml:id="d417-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed; in it Home referred to
                    President Kennedy’s letter of February 6,
                    1961 (see <ref target="#d379-f3">footnote 2, Document 379</ref>), and asked
                    President Johnson whether the US-UK
                    understanding still remained in force. (Department of State, Presidential
                    Correspondence: Lot 66 D 204)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d417-f3" n="2" xml:id="d417-f3-content">
                <p>Printed from a copy that bears this typed signature.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
